  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Showers Sunday into Monday morning. Rainfall \nwill have minimal impact on the drought.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nHigh pressure exits across the Maritimes tonight, while low \npressure begins to develop in the Mid-Atlantic region and a cold \nfront crosses Quebec Province. Clouds will increase early tonight, \nwith showers then approaching from the west late. The low will reach \nthe Gulf of Maine later Sunday, while the cold front approaches the \nSaint Lawrence Valley. Models have been trending offshore with the \nbetter upper level support and better precipitable water \nvalues. Current indications are that the heavier rains should \nremain across the waters of the Gulf of Maine, possibly clipping\nDowneast areas. Other showers are possible across northern \nareas in advance of the approaching cold front. Although \ninstability is limited, could also have isolated/scattered \nthunderstorms across northern areas Sunday afternoon/evening. \nThe low exits across the Maritimes Sunday night, while the cold \nfront crosses the region and an upper trof approaches from the \nwest. Shower chances will persist across the region Sunday \nnight. The upper trof then crosses the region Monday keeping a \nchance of mostly early showers. Rainfall with this system will \nhave minimal impact on the drought.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Patchy/areas of frost possible across northern \nareas Monday night.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nHigh pressure will build across the region behind the cold \nfront Monday, bringing clear skies and light winds. This will \nresult in favorable conditions for radiational cooling. Low \ntemperatures will range from the mid to upper 30s across much of\nnorthern Maine. Patchy/areas of frost are possible Monday night\nacross the Northern Areas but particularly in the normally \ncolder low lying locations.",
